WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.747
Let’s bring the sequence concepts together.

00:00:02.747 --> 00:00:06.042
Lists are ordered and mutable.

00:00:06.042 --> 00:00:09.942
Indexes give access to individual positions.

00:00:09.942 --> 00:00:15.907
Slices retrieve ranges. Loops let us process each value.

00:00:15.907 --> 00:00:19.509
Tuples are ordered but intended to remain fixed.

00:00:20.480 --> 00:00:27.322
You can now organize multiple values and write small workflows that summarize or filter collections.

00:00:27.322 --> 00:00:33.673
Next, dictionaries and sets will give us structures for labeled values and unique values.

00:00:34.165 --> 00:00:39.502
Module 5 moves from position-based collections to labeled and unique data.

00:00:39.502 --> 00:00:47.327
We’ll use dictionaries to represent records with meaningful keys, and sets to work with unique values, duplicates,

00:00:47.327 --> 00:00:54.970
and group comparisons. Those structures are especially useful as our examples start to look more like real datasets.
